    "raw": "{\"ruleId\":\"item-2a73bf4670f3\",\"class\":\"partial\",\"constraints\":[{\"id\":\"no-process-env-domain\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"severity\":\"high\",\"pattern\":\"process\\\\.env\",\"path\":\"src/domain/**\"},{\"id\":\"no-process-env-app\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"severity\":\"high\",\"pattern\":\"process\\\\.env\",\"path\":\"src/app/**\"},{\"id\":\"no-process-env-infra\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"severity\":\"high\",\"pattern\":\"process\\\\.env\",\"path\":\"src/infra/**\"}],\"coverage\":\"Any occurrence of the literal text process.env, in code, strings or comments, on any line of a .ts file under src/domain, src/app or src/infra in the post-change tree.\",\"residual\":\"Does the change add or modify a non-.ts file (for example .js, .tsx, .json or .md) under src/domain, src/app or src/infra that contains the text process.env?\",\"probes\":{\"violating\":[{\"patch\":\"diff --git a/src/domain/env-leak.ts b/src/domain/env-leak.ts\\nnew file mode 100644\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/domain/env-leak.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1 @@\\n+// reads process.env.FOO\\n\"},{\"patch\":\"diff --git a/src/app/env-leak.ts b/src/app/env-leak.ts\\nnew file mode 100644\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/app/env-leak.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1 @@\\n+export const port = process.env.PORT;\\n\"},{\"patch\":\"diff --git a/src/infra/env-leak.ts b/src/infra/env-leak.ts\\nnew file mode 100644\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/infra/env-leak.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1 @@\\n+export const dbUrl = process.env.DB_URL;\\n\"}],\"compliant\":{\"patch\":\"diff --git a/src/config/env.ts b/src/config/env.ts\\nnew file mode 100644\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/config/env.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1 @@\\n+export const dbUrl = process.env.DB_URL;\\n\"}},\"rationale\":\"The rule is a literal substring ban, and process.env cannot span lines, so a line regex scoped to each directory decides it for every scanned .ts file. The checker scans only src/**/*.ts. Non-.ts files in those directories, which the rule also covers, are invisible to it, so a residual remains.\"}",
